course: biology
lesson: 87
title: The reptile house: snakes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In the reptile house a big python lies so still you think it is plastic; a snake is cold-blooded, warming itself under a heat lamp instead of burning food, so it can go weeks or even months between meals.
explain: Its forked tongue flicks in and out not to sting but to smell: the two tips pick up scent from the air and tell the snake which side the smell is stronger on.
explain: In cartoons a snake charmer plays a flute and the cobra dances to the tune, and in The Jungle Book Kaa hypnotises with spinning eyes, but snakes have no outer ears and hear little of the music, and they have no eyelids, so their stare only looks spooky.
explain: The cobra sways to keep facing the moving flute, which it sees as a threat; a snake would much rather warn you off than waste its venom.
fact: Snakes are cold-blooded, so they need far less food than mammals; a big python can go months without a meal.
fact: A snake smells with its forked tongue, touching scent to a special organ in the roof of its mouth; the tongue is harmless.
fact: Reptiles get their body heat from their surroundings, so a cold snake is slow and cannot digest food properly; reptile houses have heat lamps and warm rocks.
fact: Before shedding, a snake's eyes turn cloudy as the old skin over them loosens; snakes grow all their lives, but the outer skin cannot stretch, so they peel off the old skin whole, often inside out, eye caps and all.
fact: Snakes swallow prey whole; their lower jaw is in two halves joined by stretchy tissue, so the jaws stretch rather than coming «unhinged».
fact: The king cobra is the longest venomous snake, up to about 5 metres; it spreads its neck ribs into a hood as a warning and eats mostly other snakes.
fact: Snakes have no outer ears and hear little of sounds in the air, but they feel vibrations through the ground and their jaws.
fact: Snakes have no eyelids; a clear scale covers each eye.
fact: Some harmless snakes, like the milk snake, look like the venomous coral snake, which is called mimicry; colour rhymes are not reliable, so never touch a wild snake.
fact: A rattlesnake's rattle is made of hollow rings of hard skin; a ring is added at each shed, several times a year, and old rings break off, so you cannot tell its age by counting them.
fact: Antivenom is made by giving horses tiny safe doses of venom and collecting the antibodies their blood makes; some blood pressure medicines were first found by studying venom.
fact: The smallest snakes, threadsnakes, are about 10 centimetres long and eat ant and termite larvae; the reticulated python can grow longer than 6 metres.
exercise: Why can a python live on so little food? | It is cold-blooded and does not burn food to keep warm | It soaks up sunlight through its skin and turns it into food like a plant | It drinks lots of water instead | It sleeps all year round
exercise: Why does a snake flick its tongue? | To smell the air | To sting its prey with the tips | To cool its body down | To scare away flies
exercise: What does the fork in a snake's tongue tell it? | Which side a smell is stronger on | How hot or cold the air is around it | How old the animal it is following is | Nothing, it is just a split in the tip
exercise: Why is there a heat lamp over the snake's rock? | Snakes get their body heat from their surroundings | To dry the snake's skin out so it can shed more quickly | To keep the visitors warm | To make the snake glow at night
exercise: Why do a snake's eyes look milky before shedding? | The old skin over the eyes is loosening | The snake is slowly going blind for good as it gets older | It is sleeping with its eyes open | It has eaten something bad
exercise: Why do snakes shed their skin? | They keep growing but the outer skin cannot stretch | To change their colour to suit each season of the year | To get rid of the venom | Because they are cold
exercise: How can a snake swallow an egg bigger than its head? | Its jaws and skin are loose and stretchy | It breaks the egg with its sharp teeth first | It sucks the egg through its tongue | It cannot, the sign is a joke
exercise: Why does a king cobra spread its hood? | As a warning | To glide down from the trees like a kite | To catch rain to drink | To keep the sun off
exercise: What does a king cobra mostly eat? | Other snakes | Ripe jungle bananas | Fresh green grass | Only fish from rivers
exercise: What is a cobra really reacting to when a charmer plays? | The moving flute and the charmer's body | The tune of the music and its rhythm | The smell of the flute | The words of a song
exercise: Why can't a snake hear a flute well? | It has no outer ears and hears little sound in the air | Its ears are on the end of its tail, far from the flute | The flute is too quiet | Snakes hear only high squeaks
exercise: Why is a milk snake hard to tell from a coral snake? | It looks very like the venomous one, which is called mimicry | It is really a baby coral snake that has not grown its venom yet | It changes colour like a chameleon | They are the same snake
exercise: What is a rattlesnake's rattle made of? | Hollow rings of hard skin | Small bones from the end of its tail | Little stones it swallowed long ago | Dried seeds stuck inside the tail
exercise: Can you tell a rattlesnake's age by counting rattle rings? | No, a ring is added at each shed, not each year | Yes, one new ring grows every year on its birthday | Yes, one ring every month | Only on male snakes
exercise: How is antivenom made? | Horses get tiny safe doses and their blood makes antibodies | It is squeezed from the flowers of plants that snakes avoid | It is made from the snake's skin | People drink a little venom every day
exercise: How long are the smallest snakes? | About 10 centimetres | About 1 millimetre | About 1 metre | About 10 metres
exercise: Which is the longest snake? | The reticulated python | The king cobra | The rattlesnake | The threadsnake
test: In The Jungle Book Kaa hypnotises Mowgli with spinning eyes. What would you fix? | Snakes cannot hypnotise; they have no eyelids, so they stare | Right, all pythons hypnotise their prey with their eyes before they strike | Snakes close their eyes to hypnotise | Only cobras can hypnotise people
test: 6 snakes and 2 lizards in the reptile house. how many legs | 8 | 32 | 24 | 0
test: «snake stuck its tongue out at me, it's gonna sting» What is right? | The tongue is harmless; it is just smelling the air | Yes, the tongue has a little sting at each of its two tips | Yes, the tongue sprays venom | Only the fork part can sting
test: A rattlesnake has 9 rings on its rattle. What can you say about its age? | Not much; rings are added at each shed, and some break off | It is exactly 9 years old, one ring for each year of its life | It is 9 months old | It is 90 years old
test: «just grab it, it's red, black and yellow so it's the safe one, the rhyme says» What is right? | Never touch a wild snake; colour rhymes are not reliable | The rhyme always works, everywhere in the world, so go ahead | All red snakes are harmless | Yellow means the snake is asleep
test: What would happen if the reptile house heaters broke on a cold night? | The snakes would get slow and could not digest food | The snakes would warm themselves up by shivering, like we do | Nothing, snakes make their own heat | The snakes would shed at once
test: A cartoon cobra with two little ears dances to the tune. How many outer ears does a real snake have? | None; it follows the flute's movement | Two, like a dog, but hidden under scales | One, on top of its head | Four, hidden under the hood
test: «how come the python eats once a month and I need 3 meals a day» Which answer fits? | You are warm-blooded and burn food to keep warm | Pythons sneak out to eat in secret every night | Pythons are on a diet | You are much bigger than a python
test: «snakes unhinge their jaws, the jaw just pops off» What is closer to the truth? | The jaws are loose and stretchy rather than coming apart | Yes, the jaw falls right off after every meal and grows back | No, snakes always chew their food | Yes, but only for eggs
test: Why would a cobra spread its hood instead of biting first? | Scaring you off saves its venom | It has no venom at all in its fangs | Its fangs are much too short to bite | It wants you to come and stroke its hood
test: How can something poisonous like venom help save lives? | Antivenom made from it treats bites, and it led to medicines | Drinking a little venom each day makes you stronger and braver | Venom cures colds | It cannot help anybody
test: A snake 10 centimetres long lives under the leaves. What does it most likely eat? | Ant and termite larvae | Rabbits | Fish | Birds' eggs
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a large python may subsist for months without food. | Being cold-blooded, it does not expend food on body heat | It absorbs nutrients from the air through its skin | It derives nourishment from water alone | It consumes small prey continuously and unobserved during the night
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of the forked tongue of a snake? | It gathers scent and shows on which side the scent is stronger | It injects venom into prey | It serves to cool the body during the heat of the day | It is employed to produce sound
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the snake charmer's cobra. | The snake follows the movement of the pipe, hearing little of the music | The snake dances in time with the melody | The snake is hypnotised by the charmer's voice | The snake responds to the colour of the pipe
exam: Determine the reason for which the age of a rattlesnake cannot be ascertained by counting the rings of its rattle. | A ring is added at each shedding, more than once a year, and rings break off | Rings are added once per decade | Rattlesnakes possess no rings | The rings are removed by the keepers
